time the wait for a cart face, which is the one shelf stall not in the renderer

CartFaces::sync is called from compose every frame and blocks on recv() until
every face the shelf wants on screen has been built, by two worker threads that
are the only hot threads in slot with no priority set -- audio is -10, input
-10, emu -8, these are 0, on two cores. A cart scrolling into view before its
worker finishes stalls the frame outright, and nothing measured it.

Measured over 19,200 frames of scrolling: nine waits past 2 ms, two of them
26.0 and 24.3 ms. So it is real but rare -- not the routine unevenness, which
turned out to be the sprite blit, but it is the only thing that can stall a
frame for a frame and a half with the renderer idle. It is also the leading
explanation for the gap notes/hardware.md flagged as unproven, between the
pacer's work of 36-45 ms and take>swapped of 11.

Printed per occurrence past a threshold rather than averaged: an average over
19,200 frames hides exactly the shape being looked for.

Applies on top of 0009, which owns the other end of frontend.rs's compose.

